# Cloudflare Pages production build. This is a public URL (no secrets): the
# marketplace Worker at packages/trpc-api, deployed as `pymeshub-api-staging`
# (wrangler.toml). Mirrors the fallback in client/src/lib/marketplace.ts so the
# deploy is explicit and survives that fallback changing.
#
# CORS note: the Worker only allows browser origins listed in its `CORS_ORIGINS`
# var — `https://staging.pymeshub.lat` in staging, `https://pymeshub.lat` and
# `https://www.pymeshub.lat` in production. Serving this app from any other
# origin makes every storefront request fail with no ACAO header.
VITE_MARKETPLACE_API_URL=https://api.pymeshub.lat

# The SaaS (NestJS) API — a DIFFERENT service from the Worker above.
#
# `VITE_MARKETPLACE_API_URL` names the marketplace Worker, which mounts `/trpc`,
# `/auth`, `/uploads` and nothing under `/api/*`. Everything `lib/api.ts` calls —
# `api.getMe()`, `api.login()`, and the eight pages under `/admin/workspaces`,
# `/admin/users`, `/admin/support` and their siblings — lives on the NestJS service
# in `apps/api`, which sets a global `/api` prefix (`main.ts:76`) and is deployed to
# Railway.
#
# This was unset, and the fallback in `lib/api.ts` was `""` — a valid same-origin
# base — so every one of those calls went to `pymeshub.lat`, which answered 404
# without `Access-Control-Allow-Origin`. The browser reported that as a CORS policy
# failure, so the error named CORS and the cause was a variable nobody had set.
# Client error reports were lost the same way, silently.
#
# **This value is unverified.** `apps/api/railway.json` deploys it and
# `deployment-setup.md` documents `api.pymeshub.com`, but the host Railway actually
# assigned is recorded nowhere in the repo. `curl -sI <host>/api/health` is the check.
# If it is wrong, `requireApiBase` will have accepted it and the calls will fail —
# loudly, and as a 404 from a real server rather than as a CORS error.
VITE_API_URL=https://api.pymeshub.com
